{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Executed evidence on this macOS host (Darwin 25.6.0), zsh and bash, for the guidance now in\nAGENTS.md. Every form below was run, not just written.\n\n**[1] Host inventory** — `command -v timeout gtimeout perl` prints only `/usr/bin/perl` (rc 1 in\nzsh, 0 in bash): no coreutils `timeout`, no `gtimeout`.\n\n**[2] The defect, as the section stood** — `timeout 1 echo \"payload ran\"` →\n`command not found: timeout`, `rc=127`, and `payload ran` never printed. The helper is not bounded;\nit does not exist.\n\n**[3] The same command through the documented `bound()`** → `payload ran`, `rc=0`.\n\n**[4] The bound expiring** — `( bound 3 python3 -c 'while True: pass' )` → `rc=142` after `3s`\n(SIGALRM). `pgrep -fl 'while True: pass'` afterwards: nothing.\n\n**[5] A host with no bounder at all** — same snippet under `env PATH=/tmp/emptybin`:\n`no wall-clock bounder (timeout/gtimeout/perl): not spawning` on stderr, `rc=127`, payload not\nstarted. Loud, not silent.\n\n**[6] A host that has `timeout`** (a stub first on PATH) → `[fake GNU timeout] bounding 5s: echo\npayload ran` then `payload ran`: the Linux branch is taken when it exists, so the fix does not\ntrade one universal assumption for another.\n\n**[7] The snippet verbatim, backgrounded, with its trap** — extracted programmatically from\nAGENTS.md and run: `helper=45285` is the python process itself (`ps` shows the interpreter, not a\nshell), `pgrep -P $helper` is empty, and after the script exits `pgrep -fl 'while True: pass'`\nreturns nothing. An earlier draft without `exec` recorded the wrapper subshell instead and leaked\nthe python child to PPID 1 — that leak is why `exec` is in the snippet and called out in the prose.\n\n**[8] `trap 'kill 0'` is too wide** — `( trap 'kill 0' EXIT INT TERM; sleep 1 )` inside a plain\nscript killed the script itself: `rc=143` (SIGTERM), and the statements after it never ran.\n\n**[9] `trap 'kill 0'` is also too narrow** — a wrapper `zsh -c \"perl -e 'alarm 20; exec @ARGV'\nsleep 20\"` started at pgid 77027; a subshell with its own group ran `trap 'kill 0' EXIT`; result:\n`wrapper 77044 SURVIVED the subshell's trap 'kill 0'`. Killing the recorded pid removed it.\n\n**[10] The recorded-pid trap works** — spawn, `trap 'kill \"$helper\" 2>/dev/null' EXIT INT TERM`,\nsubshell exits `rc=0` in 1s, helper reaped, caller still alive.\n\n**[11] `pgrep -f` matches text, not identity** — a sibling shell whose command line merely mentions\na token (`zsh -c \"sleep 6; echo token-ABC\"`) is returned by `pgrep -f token-ABC` from an unrelated\nshell. A waiter keyed on such a pattern waits on the wrong process. `wait \"$helper\"` on the\nrecorded pid has no such failure mode.\n\nGate carve-out preserved and extended: the section still forbids bounding `review-plan`,\n`verify-completion`, a gated close, and now `make verify`.\n\n`make lint` → exit 0. `make typecheck` → `Success: no issues found in 763 source files`, exit 0.\n`scripts/check_orphaned_load.py` reports nothing after the demonstrations.\n\nOut-of-scope duplicate found: `docs/orphaned-processes.md` still carries the same defective snippet.\nFiled as `publicized-chuffy-guanaco` (6a9d-4792-7099-4a17), linked `discovered_from`, rather than\nwidening this ticket's file impact."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788548899549049001,"uuid":"12b40aba-e87a-4bf3-b75b-2714b548361f"}